---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ActionLink from "../components/content/ActionLink.astro";
import BannerCaption from "../components/content/BannerCaption.astro";
import ContactDetails from "../components/content/ContactDetails.astro";
import ContentGrid from "../components/structure/ContentGrid.astro";
import PageHeading from "../components/structure/PageHeading.astro";
import PageBody from "../components/structure/PageBody.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../components/structure/SiteHeader.astro";
import bannerImage from "../../images/banners/cv_sarah_jkl.jpg";
---

<BaseLayout title="Support the Connections Museum and THG" description="Help preserve the history of telecommunications through donations, volunteering, and sharing your connections.">
  <SiteHeader />
  <PageBody>
    <BannerCaption image={bannerImage} alt="Sarah Autumn working on step-by-step telephone switching equipment">
      Chief Engineer Sarah Autumn working on step-by-step
      equipment in the switchroom of the "JKL" American Museum of Telephony in
      Santa Cruz, California. Photograph by
      <a href="https://www.ultraviolet.gay">Claire Violet.</a>
    </BannerCaption>
    <ContentGrid columns={2} id="donate">
      <r-cell span="row">
        <PageHeading level="h1">Donations</PageHeading>
      </r-cell>
      <r-cell span="row">
        <ActionLink href="https://www.paypal.com/donate/?hosted_button_id=WYFRCDFV4X4VW" prominence="display" variant="callout" marker="→">Please help support us!</ActionLink>
        <PageHeading level="h2" id="donation-introduction">Why donate to the Connections Museum?</PageHeading>
        <p>
          Our museum is an incredibly unique place. Our collection includes an
          unparalleled number of historically significant objects, many of which
          are either the only ones ever made, the last remaining in the entire
          world. In addition, we've been able to foster a large, diverse
          organization of intensely passionate volunteers who believe in the
          work we are doing, and who are able to ensure that all aspects of our
          mission are executed well. Our volunteers bring an expertise to the
          operations of our museums and archive, and also a bright future for
          years to come. If you're not quite sure what we're talking about,
          <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6bqt_nTwod4"
            >watch this short video to see it for yourself</a
          >, or check out
          <a
            href="https://web.archive.org/web/20250404150529/https://www.seattletimes.com/pacific-nw-magazine/from-old-phones-to-volunteers-everything-clicks-at-the-connections-museum/"
            >this feature article by the Seattle Times Pacific Northwest
            Magazine</a
          >.
        </p>
      </r-cell>
      <r-cell span="1">
        <PageHeading level="h2" id="monetary-donation">Make a Monetary Donation</PageHeading>
        <p>
          The Connections Museum is
          <a href="/thg.html#what-is-thg">a 501(c)(3) non-profit organization</a>
          that depends on the support of generous individuals and organizations
          just like you. We are lucky to have a group of amazing volunteers,
          some retired telecommunications workers and some from related
          industries, but our organization still relies on fundraising to cover
          operational expenses, advertising, and expenses related to organizing
          and preserving collections.
        </p>
        <p>
          You can contribute to the Connections Museum as a whole or to one of
          our specific locations
          <a
            href="https://www.paypal.com/donate/?hosted_button_id=WYFRCDFV4X4VW"
            >via our PayPal donation link</a
          >.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="https://www.paypal.com/ncp/payment/38L8ZNNVD79GY"
            >A donation of $35</a
          >
          entitles you to be a member of THG (<i
            ><a href="/thg.html#what-is-thg">What is THG?</a></i
          >), which includes the quarterly newsletter, Connections News, for one
          year. You can also
          <a href="https://www.paypal.com/ncp/payment/V4M53RRLQBLXW"
            >join as a Donor ($50)</a
          >
          or
          <a href="https://www.paypal.com/ncp/payment/K255JV2WWWYL8"
            >become a Supporter ($100)</a
          >. You can also renew your annual membership for the same amounts by
          clicking the links above. If you'd like to make a more substantial
          donation, <i>please</i> get in touch by e-mailing us at
          <a href="mailto:donations@connections.museum"
            >donations@connections.museum</a
          >.
        </p>
        <p>
          <i>Your contributions are tax deductible as allowed by law.</i>
        </p>
        <p><strong>
          Thank you very much for helping us preserve the history of
          telecommunications, as well as make our incredible collection lasting
          and accessible.
        </strong></p>
        <PageHeading level="h2" id="introductions">Make an Introduction</PageHeading>
        <p>
          Sometimes the most valuable resources are <u>connections</u>. We
          appreciate introductions to individuals or organizations who may be a
          good fit for current or future partnerships, and interesting tours of
          industrial facilities (telecom-related or otherwise). Contact us at
          the donations email address above if you have someone in mind.
        </p>
      </r-cell>
      <r-cell span="1">
        <PageHeading level="h2" id="equipment-donations">Donate Equipment or Historical Documents</PageHeading>
        <p>
          We have an extensive collection and a wide variety of telephone
          equipment, stations, phones, memorabilia, and other physical objects.
          If you have items to donate to the Connections Museum in Seattle or
          Denver, please contact us.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you would like to donate artifacts to the Connections Museum
          Seattle, please send a description of the items (with pictures if at
          all possible) to
          <a href="mailto:donations@connections.museum"
            >donations@connections.museum</a
          >.
          <i
            >We're especially interested in switchgear, tools, and test
            equipment!</i
          >
        </p>
        <p>
          If you would like to donate documents, photographs, and other items to
          our Archives, please send a description of the items to
          <a href="mailto:archives@connections.museum"
            >archives@connections.museum</a
          >.
        </p>
        <p>
          If the documents are of a technical nature, relate to the history of
          the Pacific Northwest, Seattle, or most especially the Panel Switch
          (<i>anything marked "Full Mechanical"</i>) then please instead contact
          <a href="mailto:library@connections.museum"
            >library@connections.museum</a
          >. While we don't yet have a proper archival environment in Seattle,
          we still maintain an extensive collection of printed material related
          to the history of telephony, and especially technical material
          required for maintaining our equipment, which we try our best to make
          available to the broader telephone collector community.
          <a href="/seattle/library.html"
            >More information about our efforts therein are provided here</a
          >.
        </p>
        <PageHeading level="h3" id="mailing_address">Our Mailing Address</PageHeading>
        <p>
          Letters of appreciation and personal stories of telecommunications are
          always welcome. If you'd like to mail us documents or artifacts,
          please be sure to send us an email beforehand to ensure your donation
          is properly accessed into our collection.
        </p>
        <ContentGrid columns={2}>
          <r-cell span="1"><ContactDetails name="Connections Museum Denver, Telecommunications History Group & THG Archives" address="PO Box 8719, Denver, CO 80201-8719" /></r-cell>
          <r-cell span="1"><ContactDetails name="Connections Museum Seattle" address="PO Box 81103, Seattle, WA 98108" /></r-cell>
        </ContentGrid>
      </r-cell>
      <r-cell span="row"><hr /></r-cell>
    </ContentGrid>
    <ContentGrid columns={2} id="volunteer">
      <r-cell span="row"><PageHeading level="h2" scale="display">Volunteering</PageHeading></r-cell>
      <r-cell span="1">
        <p>
          As in many non-profit organizations, our volunteers are our life
          blood, keeping the tours moving, the switches maintained, and the
          archives in good order. Volunteers are essential to everything we do.
        </p>
        <p>
          Volunteer duties may include research, preservation, engineering,
          heavy-lifting, inventory, digitization, database entry and management,
          repair of antique equipment, tour conducting, educational speaking,
          and oral history collection. You'll have the option to explore one
          aspect of our collection more deeply, or do something new each week!
          Many of our volunteers are able to self-direct their own projects to
          improve our collections.
        </p>
        <p><strong>
          If you would like to volunteer at the Connections Museum in Seattle,
          please send an email to
          <a href="mailto:volunteer@connections.museum"
            >volunteer@connections.museum</a
          >.
        </strong></p>
        <p><strong>
          If you would like to volunteer at the Connections Museum in Denver,
          Arvada, or the Denver Archives, please send an email to
          <a href="mailto:denver@connections.museum"
            >denver@connections.museum</a
          >.
        </strong></p>
      </r-cell>
      <r-cell span="1">
        <PageHeading level="h3" id="why-volunteer">Why Volunteer?</PageHeading>
        <p>
          We depend on volunteers for a myriad of tasks and projects. If you
          have time to donate and are in the Denver or Seattle areas, let us
          know and we’d be happy to get you involved!
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>You can utilize your existing knowledge or learn new skills.</li>
          <li>
            There are opportunities to attend professional meetings and
            seminars.
          </li>
          <li>
            You'll find camaraderie with people of similar interests, and a
            diverse and inclusive community of other volunteers.
          </li>
          <li>
            You'll have access to many of the objects in our archives and
            physical collections. Their care and preservation will be entrusted
            with you. In many cases, you'll be able to use and work on them
            directly!
          </li>
          <li>
            <i
              >Please note that telephone company experience, or any kind of
              technical background is <b>not</b> required to volunteer!</i
            >
            Many of our volunteers started with no experience in field of
            telecommunications, electrical engineering, mechanical engineering,
            or archival preservation, and still make valuable contributions to
            our organization.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </r-cell>
    </ContentGrid>
  </PageBody>
</BaseLayout>
